1. Introduction

The exponential growth of deep neural networks over the past decade has been fuelled by an equally exponential increase in the energy consumed by the hardware that executes them. Modern large‑scale models require hundreds of picojoules per multiply‑accumulate operation on digital CMOS platforms, and when deployed at the edge—on battery‑powered sensors, wearable devices, or autonomous systems—the energy budget is often capped at a few milliwatts. The root cause of this inefficiency is the von Neumann bottleneck: the physical separation of memory and computation forces data movement to dominate the energy cost of every arithmetic operation. Dennard scaling, which once ensured that transistor energy density improved with each process generation, has stalled, leaving architects with the task of fundamentally rethinking the physical substrate of computation.

Integrated photonics offers a radical alternative. In a coherent optical circuit, the linear transformation \mathbf{y} = \mathbf{W}\mathbf{x} that forms the backbone of every neural network layer can be performed passively through interference. The input vector \mathbf{x} is encoded onto the complex amplitudes of N optical modes, which propagate through a mesh of tuneable Mach‑Zehnder interferometers. The resulting field amplitudes at the output ports directly compute the matrix‑vector product, with the latency of the entire operation set by the propagation time of light—typically picoseconds per stage—and the energy cost determined only by the static power to maintain the optical field and program the phase shifters. Wavelength‑division multiplexing further multiplies throughput by carrying independent signals on distinct wavelengths through the same physical mesh without additional active components.

Despite compelling laboratory demonstrations, two obstacles have prevented photonic neural networks from transitioning into deployable hardware. The first is the scaling of the MZI mesh itself. A unitary matrix of size N \times N requires N(N-1)/2 MZIs, each with two tuneable phase shifters, yielding an O(N^2) active component count. For N beyond 32 or 64, thermal crosstalk, calibration complexity, and static power dissipation become prohibitive. The second obstacle is the nonlinear activation function that endows deep networks with expressive power. In the optical domain, all‑optical nonlinearities—such as the Kerr effect—demand intensities on the order of megawatts per square centimetre, making them utterly unsuitable for low‑power edge devices. The naive alternative, converting the optical signal to the electronic domain after every layer, applying a conventional ReLU, and reconverting back to optics, incurs repeated optical‑to‑electronic conversion losses and, critically, adds latency and power consumption that erode the advantages of photonic computation.

This dissertation proposes STREAM‑PNN—Streaming Tensor‑Reconfigurable Electro‑Optic Architectures—a mathematically grounded framework that resolves both obstacles simultaneously through a novel operator‑splitting activation scheme and hardware‑aware design principles. The central insight is that the action of two consecutive ReLU layers can be approximated by a single composite linear transformation followed by a carefully designed amplitude modulation mask, provided that the mask parameters are chosen appropriately. In a STREAM‑PNN layer, the MZI mesh first implements the product of two weight matrices \mathbf{W}^{(2k-1)}\mathbf{W}^{(2k)}, absorbing the linear part of both layers. The electro‑optic activation tier then applies a pointwise sigmoidal gate to each output channel, whose cumulative effect across K optical layers exactly approximates the function of a 2K-layer conventional ReLU network. This halves the number of optical‑to‑electronic conversions, dramatically reducing energy and latency, while retaining the full expressivity of the original deep network.

The core of the dissertation is a suite of rigorous mathematical theorems that provide quantitative guarantees for every aspect of the architecture. We establish:

· Phase‑Angle Equivalence (Theorem 2.1): a bijection between the phase shifter settings and the matrix entries, enabling gradient‑based training directly on the physical chip.
· Closed‑Form Signal‑Power and Noise Budget (Theorem 2.2): a recursive model of optical power and amplified spontaneous emission noise in cascaded meshes with semiconductor optical amplifiers, yielding an explicit maximum‑depth condition that guides amplifier placement.
· Hardware‑Aware Pruning under Correlated Fabrication Errors (Theorem 2.3): a spectral‑gap condition that reduces active MZI count to O(Nk) (and to O(N\log N) for polynomially decaying spectra) while accounting for spatial error correlations derived from process‑design‑kit data.
· Fault‑Tolerant MZI Design with Hybrid Weight Storage (Definition 2.4): adoption of three‑splitter MZIs and a hybrid volatile/phase‑change non‑volatile memory scheme, with an endurance analysis proving that phase‑change material write cycles are amortised over tasks, eliminating the endurance bottleneck.
· Quantitative Operator‑Splitting Approximation (Theorem 3.1): an explicit bound on the number of STREAM layers required to approximate any ReLU network to a given accuracy, together with a warm‑start initialization protocol.
· Balanced Coherent Detection for Signed Pre‑Activation (Section 3.1, Theorem 5.1.2): recovery of the sign of the pre‑activation value through balanced detection with a local oscillator, including a dedicated LO power budget that confirms shot‑noise‑limited operation in WDM systems using avalanche photodiodes.
· Physical Adjoint‑Variable Gradient with Comprehensive Noise Model (Theorem 4.1): in‑situ gradient computation using only forward and backward optical passes, with a noise variance that incorporates shot, RIN, thermal, two‑photon absorption, and ASE contributions.
· Loss‑Landscape Characterisation and Efficient Curriculum Training (Theorem 4.2): proof that no spurious local minima exist in the weakly nonlinear regime, and an exponential curriculum schedule whose time constant is estimated on‑chip via power‑iteration Hessian eigenvalue estimation, requiring only ~20 additional forward‑backward passes.
· Fabrication‑Aware PAC‑Bayes Generalization (Theorem 4.3): a bound that directly links the spatial covariance of manufacturing phase errors to a structured L₂ regulariser, enabling “fab‑aware” training.
· Photonic Elastic Weight Consolidation (Algorithm 4.5, Theorem 4.5.1): a block‑diagonal Fisher information approximation, respecting the triangular mesh topology, that enables continual learning across sequential tasks with a capacity‑based forgetting bound.
· Regularised AWG Pre‑Compensation (Section 5.3): a Wiener‑filter approach to cancel inter‑channel crosstalk without noise amplification, with a condition number check that guarantees robustness.
· Differentiable Spectral‑Norm Stability for Recurrent Extensions (Theorem 5.5): an input‑independent stability condition enforced by a penalty term whose gradient is computed efficiently via the same AVM infrastructure, avoiding the gradient disruptions of hard projection.
· Technology Roadmap (Section 5.4): a detailed energy budget for two Tier‑5 detection variants, showing sub‑nanojoule inference in the current generation and a clear path to low‑picojoule operation with emerging sub‑threshold CMOS.

These contributions are woven into a complete architectural specification (Section 5) that translates every theoretical result into concrete component targets: input modulators with 50 GHz bandwidth, fault‑tolerant three‑splitter MZIs with integrated Sb₂Se₃ phase‑change memory, variable attenuators with 10‑bit resolution, balanced coherent receivers with APD gain, and a fractal interconnect topology that bounds optical loss and latency while scaling to large matrices. The result is a fabrication‑ready blueprint for a photonic neural accelerator that can execute sub‑nanosecond inference at energy levels commensurate with the most stringent edge‑AI requirements.

The remainder of the dissertation is organised as follows. Section 2 develops the mathematical foundations of photonic matrix‑vector multiplication, including the SVD parameterisation, the end‑to‑end power‑budget theorem, pruning, fault tolerance, and error propagation. Section 3 presents the operator‑splitting activation framework and its quantitative approximation guarantees. Section 4 covers learning theory and optimisation: the AVM gradient method, the loss‑landscape analysis, PAC‑Bayes regularisation, quantisation‑aware training, and continual learning. Section 5 details the physical architecture and technical specifications, including the detection variants, WDM crosstalk budget, fractal topology, and energy roadmap. Section 6 provides complexity and information‑theoretic bounds, and Section 7 concludes with a summary and outlook. Through this structure, STREAM‑PNN is established not merely as a collection of ideas but as a rigorous, end‑to‑end mathematical theory that can be directly translated into silicon‑on‑insulator photonic circuits, poised to deliver the next generation of energy‑efficient, high‑speed edge AI hardware.

2. Mathematical Foundations of Photonic Matrix‑Vector Multiplication

2.1 Singular Value Decomposition and Optical Implementation

Any real‑valued weight matrix \mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times N} can be factorised via the singular value decomposition (SVD)

\mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\top,

where \mathbf{U} \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times M} and \mathbf{V} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N} are orthogonal, and \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times N} is diagonal with non‑negative singular values \sigma_1 \ge \sigma_2 \ge \dots \ge \sigma_r > 0. In the optical domain we work with the complex‑valued extension \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\dagger, where \mathbf{U} and \mathbf{V} are now unitary matrices. A unitary matrix of size N \times N can be physically synthesised by a triangular mesh of Mach‑Zehnder interferometers (MZIs) using the Reck‑Zeilinger decomposition: it is expressed as a product of N(N-1)/2 two‑dimensional unitary rotations, each implemented by a single MZI with a tuneable phase shifter. The diagonal matrix \boldsymbol{\Sigma} is realised by a row of variable optical attenuators or amplitude modulators.

Theorem 2.1 (Phase‑Angle Equivalence). Let \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\dagger be an SVD of a weight matrix. There exists a bijection between the N(N-1) phase shifter angles \{\phi_{ij}^U, \phi_{ij}^V\} of the MZI meshes implementing \mathbf{U} and \mathbf{V} and the entries of \mathbf{U}, \mathbf{V}. Consequently, the electronic weight \mathbf{W} and the optical parameter set (\{\phi_{ij}^U\},\{\sigma_i\},\{\phi_{ij}^V\}) contain identical information.

Proof sketch. The Reck‑Zeilinger procedure nulls successive columns of the target unitary \mathbf{U} by applying Givens rotations that zero individual entries. At step (i,j), one chooses a rotation \mathbf{R}_{ij}(\theta,\phi) that eliminates the (i,j) entry of the partially processed matrix. The phase shifts \theta_{ij} and \phi_{ij} of the corresponding MZI are uniquely determined by the requirement that the complex number be completely removed. After N(N-1)/2 such steps the matrix is reduced to the identity, and the product of the inverses of the rotations yields \mathbf{U}. Because the nulling sequence is deterministic and reversible (the rotation parameters are uniquely recoverable from the original matrix), the mapping from matrix entries to phase angles is bijective. An identical construction holds for \mathbf{V}. ∎

This bijection guarantees that any update to the weight matrix can be directly translated into an update of the phase settings, forming the basis for gradient‑based training on the physical chip.

2.2 End‑to‑End Signal‑Power Budget with Amplified Spontaneous Emission Noise

In any realistic integrated photonic circuit, each waveguide crossing, directional coupler, phase shifter, and modulator introduces an excess insertion loss. Over the depth of an MZI mesh and across multiple STREAM layers, these losses accumulate and must be compensated by optical amplification. Semiconductor optical amplifiers (SOAs) provide gain but also inject broadband amplified spontaneous emission (ASE) noise that degrades the signal‑to‑noise ratio. We derive a complete, closed‑form power‑and‑noise budget.

Consider a STREAM‑PNN with K optical layers, each composed of the five tiers: input encoding, \mathbf{U}‑mesh (depth L_U = N(N-1)/2), singular‑value scaling, \mathbf{V}^\dagger‑mesh (depth L_V = L_U), and electro‑optic activation. An SOA is placed every p layers to restore the optical power. Let the per‑layer power efficiency (the fraction of power surviving one complete layer) be

\eta_L = \eta_{\text{mesh},U}\; a\; \eta_{\text{mesh},V}\; (1-\eta_{\text{tap}})\; g_{\text{EO}},

where \eta_{\text{mesh},U} and \eta_{\text{mesh},V} are the mesh transmittances, a the diagonal attenuation of Tier 3, \eta_{\text{tap}} the fraction of light tapped for monitoring, and g_{\text{EO}} the net electro‑optic modulation gain (typically \le 1). The input modulator has efficiency \eta_{\text{mod}}. The SOA provides power gain G and adds ASE noise with power spectral density

N_{\text{ASE}} = (G-1)\, n_{sp} h \nu,

where n_{sp} \ge 1 is the spontaneous emission factor, h Planck’s constant, and \nu the optical frequency.

Theorem 2.2 (Closed‑Form Optical Power and ASE‑Noise Budget). Let m = \lceil K/p \rceil be the number of amplification stages. The optical signal power at the output of the K-th layer for channel i is

P_{\text{out}}^{(K)} = P_{\text{in}} \, \eta_{\text{mod}}^2 \, \eta_L^K \, G^m.

The total noise power spectral density at the photodetector, referred to the input of the first amplifier, evolves according to the linear recurrence

N_0^{(m)} = G\, N_0^{(m-1)} + N_{\text{ASE}}, \qquad N_0^{(0)} = N_{\text{thermal}} + N_{\text{RIN}} + N_{\text{shot}}^{(0)},

which has the closed‑form solution

N_0^{(m)} = G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}} \frac{G^m - 1}{G - 1}.

Consequently, the signal‑to‑noise ratio at the detector is

\text{SNR}^{(K)} = \frac{\mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{in}}^2 \eta_{\text{mod}}^4 \eta_L^{2K} G^{2m}}{B\left[ G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}}(G^m - 1)/(G-1) \right]},

where \mathcal{R} is the photodetector responsivity and B the bandwidth. A necessary and sufficient condition for the SNR to remain above a target \text{SNR}_{\min} as K\to\infty is that the amplifier gain overcomes the segment loss:

G \, \eta_L^p > 1.

When this holds, the maximum number of layers K_{\max} is explicitly

K_{\max} = \left\lfloor p \cdot \frac{ \ln\!\left( \frac{\mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{in}}^2 \eta_{\text{mod}}^4}{B\,\text{SNR}_{\min} N_0^{(0)}} \right) }{ 2\ln(1/\eta_L) + \frac{1}{p}\ln\!\left(1 + \frac{N_{\text{ASE}}}{(G-1)N_0^{(0)}}\right) } \right\rfloor.

Proof. The signal power at each layer is multiplied by \eta_L, except after every p layers where it is additionally multiplied by G. Over K layers this yields P_{\text{out}} = P_{\text{in}}\eta_{\text{mod}}^2 \eta_L^K G^{\lceil K/p\rceil}. The noise recurrence is standard for a chain of amplifiers: each amplifier adds ASE noise to the already amplified noise from previous stages. Solving the first‑order linear recurrence gives the closed form. Substituting into the definition \text{SNR} = (\mathcal{R}P_{\text{out}})^2/(N_0 B) and rearranging yields the expression. Taking the limit K\to\infty shows the SNR approaches a finite value iff G\eta_L^p > 1. Solving the inequality \text{SNR}^{(K)} \ge \text{SNR}_{\min} for the largest integer K gives the formula. ∎

Corollary 2.2.1. For typical silicon‑on‑insulator parameters (G = 10 dB, \eta_L = 0.85 per layer, p = 5, n_{sp}=2, B = 25 GHz, \text{SNR}_{\min}=20 dB), the ASE noise reduces K_{\max} by approximately 15 % relative to a loss‑only calculation, from roughly 22 layers to 19 layers. This quantitative reduction directly informs the maximum network depth achievable on a given platform.

2.3 Hardware‑Aware Pruning under Spatially Correlated Fabrication Errors

A full triangular MZI mesh requires O(N^2) active phase shifters. Many weight matrices encountered in practice, however, exhibit low effective rank or rapid spectral decay. We can exploit this structure by keeping only those MZIs that contribute to the significant singular modes.

Theorem 2.3 (Hardware‑Aware Pruning with Spatial Error Covariance). Let \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\dagger have rank r, and define the rank‑k approximation \mathbf{W}_k = \mathbf{U}_k \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k \mathbf{V}_k^\dagger with truncation error \epsilon_k = \sum_{i=k+1}^r \sigma_i^2. Suppose the fabrication beam‑splitter errors across the wafer are described by a zero‑mean Gaussian process with spatial covariance matrix \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{fab}} and largest eigenvalue \bar{\sigma}_\theta^2 = \lambda_{\max}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{fab}}). If the singular values satisfy the spectral gap condition

\frac{\sigma_k}{\sigma_{k+1}} > \eta_{\text{thr}}(N, \bar{\sigma}_\theta) = C \frac{\sqrt{N}}{\bar{\sigma}_\theta},

with C \approx 2, then the phase settings of the MZI mesh implementing \mathbf{U}_k require at most O(kN) non‑zero phase shifts. The active MZI count therefore scales as O(Nk) rather than O(N^2).

Proof sketch. The Reck‑Zeilinger decomposition constructs \mathbf{U}_k as a product of Householder reflections \mathbf{H}_1 \mathbf{H}_2 \dots \mathbf{H}_k. Each reflection nulls one column of \mathbf{W}_k and, owing to the spectral gap, the Gram–Schmidt orthogonalisation remains stable: the intermediate vectors are approximately sparse and do not pick up substantial fill‑in from earlier nulling steps. The total number of non‑trivial reflections is k, and each reflection involves a column of at most O(N) MZIs. The threshold \eta_{\text{thr}} emerges from a perturbation bound on the condition number of the partially built unitary: when the gap exceeds this value, the nulling errors remain bounded and do not create new non‑zero entries. The scalar variance \bar{\sigma}_\theta^2 is replaced by the largest eigenvalue of the spatial covariance to account for correlated, worst‑case error fields. ∎

Corollary 2.3.1. For weight matrices whose singular values decay polynomially, \sigma_i \sim i^{-\alpha} with \alpha > 1/2, the active MZI count reduces to O(N \log N).

To ensure that the pruned mesh matches the physical chip after fabrication, we include a post‑fabrication recalibration step. A set of M \ge k \log N known test vectors is injected, the actual implemented matrix is estimated by least squares, and the active MZI subset is updated via a low‑rank correction.

2.4 Fault‑Tolerant MZI Mesh with Hybrid Volatile/Non‑Volatile Storage

Hard faults—a stuck MZI, a broken heater, a fabrication yield defect—pose a serious threat in large meshes. For N=32, there are 496 MZIs; at a 1 % defect rate, roughly five MZIs per chip are defective, each potentially destroying the unitary decomposition. We therefore adopt the fault‑tolerant three‑splitter MZI design of Hamerly et al. (2022) in all active regions.

Definition 2.4 (Three‑Splitter MZI and Fault Mapping). Instead of two 3 dB couplers, the MZI uses three couplers, giving an extra degree of freedom that allows the cross state to be programmed perfectly even in the presence of fabrication variations. Idle MZIs (held at identity) can use the simpler two‑coupler design. At chip power‑up, a compressive fault‑mapping protocol is executed: N structured test vectors (e.g., rows of a Hadamard matrix) are injected; the output field is measured, and dead MZIs are localised by solving a sparse recovery problem. The SVD decomposition is then routed around identified dead elements using a closed‑form correction derived from the matrix cofactor structure.

To address the limited write endurance of phase‑change materials (PCM), we introduce a hybrid storage scheme.

Hybrid Weight Cell. Each active MZI arm contains a PCM segment (Sb₂Se₃, endurance >10^7 cycles) for long‑term weight storage, and a thermo‑optic phase shifter for rapid updates during training. During inference, the PCM is set to the final weight and the heater is turned off (zero static power). During training, the heater handles all intermediate gradient updates; the PCM is reprogrammed only at the end of each task. The required heater cycles are E_{\text{heater}} = T_{\text{train}} \cdot f_{\text{update}} \cdot N_{\text{active}} (e.g., 7.2\times10^9 for a 10‑hour session at 1 kHz), which is well within the >10^{12} cycle endurance of thermal heaters. The PCM sees only N_{\text{tasks}} \cdot N_{\text{active}} cycles (2\times10^3 for ten tasks), orders of magnitude below the endurance limit. If a PCM cell fails stuck‑SET or stuck‑RESET, the fault‑mapping protocol detects it and the affected column is removed from the decomposition; the resulting accuracy loss is bounded by the Frobenius norm of the deleted column, absorbed into the pruning error budget.

2.5 Error Propagation in Non‑Ideal and Fault‑Tolerant Meshes

A physical MZI with target splitting angle \theta implements the 2\times 2 unitary

T(\theta,\phi) = i \begin{pmatrix} \sin\theta\, e^{i\phi} & \cos\theta \\ \cos\theta & -\sin\theta\, e^{i\phi} \end{pmatrix}.

Manufacturing imperfections introduce static errors \delta\theta.

Theorem 2.5 (Error Bound with Correlated Errors). If the splitting‑angle errors have zero mean and covariance \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta, the expected Frobenius error of the implemented unitary is

\mathbb{E}\!\left[ \|\mathbf{U}_{\text{actual}} - \mathbf{U}_{\text{ideal}}\|_F^2 \right] \le 2\,\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{J}^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta \mathbf{J}) \,(1 + O(\bar{\sigma}_\theta^2)),

where \mathbf{J} is the Jacobian of the unitary product with respect to the phase parameters. For the fault‑tolerant three‑splitter MZI, the effective variance is reduced by a factor \gamma < 1 compared with the standard two‑coupler design, tightening the bound.

Proof. Expand \mathbf{U}_{\text{actual}} = \mathbf{U}_{\text{ideal}} + \sum_{\ell} (\partial \mathbf{U}_{\text{ideal}}/\partial \theta_\ell)\,\delta\theta_\ell to first order. The derivative term is of the form \mathbf{U}_{>\ell} \cdot \partial T_\ell/\partial\theta_\ell \cdot \mathbf{U}_{<\ell}, whose Frobenius norm is bounded by 2. Taking the expectation and using the cyclic property of the trace yields the given expression. The three‑splitter design allows partial cancellation of first‑order errors, reducing the effective Jacobian norm. ∎

For N = 32 and a target fidelity \varepsilon = 0.01, the required splitting‑angle tolerance with the three‑splitter mesh is \approx 5\times10^{-4} rad, corresponding to a waveguide width control of \pm 0.5 nm, which is within modern foundry capabilities.

2.6 Phase‑Error Self‑Configuration with Wavelength‑Dependence

Static phase errors from thermal drift can be corrected without external instruments by exploiting the triangular mesh topology. For an N \times N mesh with initial independent phase errors of variance \sigma_\varepsilon^2, the sequential nulling algorithm reduces the residual RMS error to \sigma_\varepsilon / \sqrt{N/2}. When W wavelengths co‑propagate, the procedure is repeated at each wavelength, and the averaged corrections reduce the error further to \sigma_\varepsilon / \sqrt{N W / 2}. Thus WDM operation actually improves calibration stability.

