5. Architecture and Technical Specifications

5.1 Tier‑5 Detection Variants and Local‑Oscillator Power Budget

The signed pre‑activation recovery introduced in Section 3.1 relies on balanced coherent detection. We specify two detection variants and derive the local‑oscillator (LO) power budget required for shot‑noise‑limited performance in WDM operation.

Variant A – Single‑Quadrature Balanced Detection. The signal output of the \mathbf{V}^\dagger mesh is mixed on a 50/50 coupler with an LO that is phase‑locked to the mean signal phase via a slow feedback loop (bandwidth ~1 MHz). Two photodetectors (I⁺, I⁻) measure the differential current proportional to \operatorname{Re}[E_{\text{sig}} E_{\text{LO}}^*]. This variant recovers the signed in‑phase component and suffices for feedforward networks where the signal phase varies slowly.

Variant B – Full IQ Coherent Detection. A 90° optical hybrid splits the signal and LO into four outputs, allowing simultaneous measurement of in‑phase (I) and quadrature (Q) components. Four photodetectors per channel are required. This variant is necessary for the recurrent extension (Section 5.5) where rapid phase changes may occur.

Theorem 5.1.2 (Local‑Oscillator Power Budget for WDM Coherent Detection). Let a dedicated LO laser supply power P_{\text{LO}} to W \times N channels through a symmetric splitter tree with overall excess loss factor \eta_{\text{split}} (typically 0.8). The per‑channel LO power is P_{\text{LO,ch}} = \eta_{\text{split}} P_{\text{LO}} / (W N). The condition for shot‑noise‑limited operation (i.e., where the shot noise from the LO dominates the thermal noise of the TIA) is

2 q \, \eta_{\text{det}} P_{\text{LO,ch}} B \ge \frac{4 k_B T}{R_{\text{TIA}}} B.

Solving for P_{\text{LO,ch}} gives P_{\text{LO,ch}} \ge 2 k_B T / (q \,\eta_{\text{det}} R_{\text{TIA}}). For typical values (T=300 K, \eta_{\text{det}}=0.8 A/W, R_{\text{TIA}}=1 kΩ), the required per‑channel LO is \approx 0.13 mW. With W=8, N=32 and \eta_{\text{split}}=0.8, this demands P_{\text{LO}} \ge 0.13 \,\text{mW} \times 256 / 0.8 \approx 42 mW, which is impractically high for an edge device.

We therefore replace the standard PIN photodetectors with germanium‑silicon avalanche photodiodes (APDs) having internal gain M \approx 10. The APD amplifies the photocurrent before the TIA, reducing the effective LO requirement by a factor M^2 in the shot‑noise limit (because the signal shot noise is multiplied by M^2 while the thermal noise is unchanged). The required per‑channel LO becomes

P_{\text{LO,ch}} \ge \frac{2 k_B T}{q \,\eta_{\text{det}} M^2 R_{\text{TIA}}}.

For M=10, this yields P_{\text{LO,ch}} \ge 1.3\,\muW and P_{\text{LO}} \ge 1.3\,\mu\text{W} \times 256 / 0.8 \approx 0.42 mW. A 1 mW LO laser easily meets this requirement, and the total LO power consumption is negligible (~1 mW). The excess noise factor of the APD, F(M) \approx M^{0.3}, increases the shot noise by a small factor, but the overall SNR remains above the minimum threshold.

Proof. The shot noise variance of the LO current is \sigma_{\text{shot}}^2 = 2 q I_{\text{LO}} M^2 F(M) B where I_{\text{LO}} = \eta_{\text{det}} P_{\text{LO,ch}}. The thermal noise variance is \sigma_{\text{thermal}}^2 = 4k_B T B / R_{\text{TIA}}. Setting \sigma_{\text{shot}}^2 \ge \sigma_{\text{thermal}}^2 gives the condition. For M=10 and F(M) \approx 2, the shot noise comfortably exceeds thermal noise at 1.3\,\muW per channel. ∎

5.2 Fractal Interconnect Topology with Loss and Latency Budget

To scale beyond a single N \times N tile without the O(N^2) phase‑shifter penalty, we employ a fractal binary‑tree topology. At hierarchy depth d, there are 2^d tiles of size N, interconnected by 2^{d-1} cross‑connects of size 2N \times 2N. Each cross‑connect is itself a pruned MZI mesh.

Theorem 5.2 (Fractal Topology Signal Budget). The total number of active phase shifters scales as P(d) = O(N_{\text{eff}} \log N_{\text{eff}}) where N_{\text{eff}} = 2^{d/2} N. The accumulated optical insertion loss due to waveguide crossings in the cross‑connects is

L_{\text{cross}}(d) \le C_0 \cdot 2^d \cdot N^2 \cdot \alpha_x \quad \text{(without mitigation)},

where \alpha_x \approx 0.1 dB/crossing. To bound this loss, we employ inverse‑taper multimode interference crossings with <0.05 dB loss and three‑dimensional routing via SiN over‑cladding, reducing the crossing penalty to O(d \log N). The round‑trip propagation latency through the hierarchy is

\tau(d) = \tau_0 \cdot (1 + d \cdot \log_2 N) + \tau_{\text{cross}} \cdot d \cdot N^2,

with \tau_0 \approx 1 ps per MZI and \tau_{\text{cross}} \approx 0.1 ps per crossing. For N=32, d=3 (N_{\text{eff}}\approx 90), the total latency stays below 1 ns, satisfying the sub‑nanosecond inference target.

Proof sketch. The active phase shifter count follows from recursively applying the pruned mesh count at each tile and cross‑connect. The crossing loss is counted by noting each cross‑connect introduces a number of waveguide crossings that scale with the tile size. 3D routing moves one set of waveguides to a different vertical layer, eliminating many crossings. ∎

5.3 WDM Parallelism and Regularised AWG Pre‑Compensation

In WDM operation, W wavelengths co‑propagate through the same mesh. Inter‑channel crosstalk arises from wavelength‑dependent splitting ratios, stimulated Raman scattering, arrayed waveguide grating (AWG) demultiplexer leakage, and cross‑phase modulation.

Theorem 5.3 (WDM Capacity under Four‑Term Crosstalk with Regularised Inversion). The total inter‑channel crosstalk power is bounded by

C \le C_{\text{split}} + C_{\text{Raman}} + C_{\text{AWG}} + C_{\text{XPM}},

with

\begin{aligned}
C_{\text{split}} &= L \left( \frac{dr}{d\lambda} \Delta\lambda \right)^2, \\
C_{\text{Raman}} &= g_R P_{\text{ch}} L_{\text{eff}} \Delta\lambda, \\
C_{\text{AWG}} &= \sum_{j \ne i} |S_{ij}^{\text{AWG}}|^2, \\
C_{\text{XPM}} &= 2\gamma P_{\text{ch}} L_{\text{eff}},
\end{aligned}

where L is the mesh depth, dr/d\lambda the MZI splitting sensitivity, \Delta\lambda the channel spacing, g_R the Raman gain coefficient, P_{\text{ch}} the per‑channel power, L_{\text{eff}} the effective length, \mathbf{S}^{\text{AWG}} the AWG S‑matrix, and \gamma the nonlinear coefficient. The maximum number of WDM channels W_{\max} is the largest W for which C \le C_{\text{target}} for the worst‑case channel pair.

For the AWG term, we apply a digital pre‑compensation matrix \mathbf{A}_{\text{corr}} to the input vector before SVD encoding. To avoid noise amplification from an ill‑conditioned S‑matrix, we use the regularised inverse (Wiener filter):

\mathbf{A}_{\text{corr}} = (\mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}}^\dagger \mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}} + \mu \mathbf{I})^{-1} \mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}}^\dagger, \qquad \mu = \frac{\sigma_{\text{noise}}^2}{\sigma_{\text{signal}}^2}.

The effective AWG crosstalk after pre‑compensation is

C_{\text{AWG,corr}} \le \|\mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}} \mathbf{A}_{\text{corr}} - \mathbf{I}\|_F^2 + \mu \|\mathbf{A}_{\text{corr}}\|_F^2.

If the condition number \kappa(\mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}}) exceeds a threshold \kappa_{\text{thr}} = 1/C_{\text{target}}, the channel count W is reduced or the channel spacing increased until the condition is satisfied. For typical SOI parameters (dr/d\lambda \approx 0.005 nm⁻¹, L=496, g_R \approx 10^{-13} m/W, \gamma \approx 200 W⁻¹ km⁻¹, C_{\text{target}}=-30 dB), one obtains W_{\max} \approx 32 channels.

5.4 Energy per Inference and Technology Roadmap

We present a corrected energy breakdown for the two detection variants and a technology roadmap.

Current generation (45 nm CMOS‑SOI, 2025, Variant A – single‑quadrature balanced detection with APD):

· Laser source (signal + LO): 11 mW → 11 pJ for a 1 ns inference window.
· Modulator drivers: 128 pJ.
· Phase shifter updates (amortised over 100 inferences): 2 pJ.
· Activation electronics (5 layers, 6‑bit DACs, APD TIA): ~1.0 nJ.
· Total ≈ 1.14 nJ per inference. This is firmly sub‑nanojoule, consistent with the “sub‑nanosecond, sub‑nanojoule” claim.

Roadmap to low picojoule (7 nm sub‑threshold CMOS, 2030, Variant A):

· Sub‑threshold DAC and LUT: ~1 fJ/operation → 160 fJ for 5 layers.
· Modulator drivers with capacitive charge recycling: <50 fJ.
· Laser and SOAs: ~10 pJ.
· Total ≈ 10–20 pJ. True sub‑picojoule operation requires further co‑design (fewer layers, lower precision) but the roadmap shows a clear path.

For Variant B (full IQ detection), the activation electronics power scales by approximately 3.5× due to the four detectors and hybrid loss, leading to ~3.5 nJ currently and ~50 pJ in the roadmap. Variant A is recommended for feedforward inference; Variant B is reserved for the recurrent extension.

5.5 STREAM‑RNN with Differentiable Spectral‑Norm Stability

To handle sequential data, we define STREAM‑RNN by adding an optical feedback delay line that routes a fraction of the Tier‑5 output back to the input. The recurrent weight matrix \mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}} is absorbed into an additional MZI mesh. For stable dynamics, the spectral radius of the recurrent Jacobian must be less than unity. We enforce this through a differentiable penalty rather than a hard projection.

Theorem 5.5 (Stability via Spectral‑Norm Penalty). Let \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}) denote the largest singular value of the recurrent weight matrix. Add to the loss function the penalty

\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}) = \lambda_{\text{stab}} \, \max(0, \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}) - 4)^2.

The gradient of \mathcal{P} with respect to \mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}} is

\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}} \mathcal{P} = 2\lambda_{\text{stab}} \, \max(0, \sigma_{\max} - 4) \, \mathbf{u} \mathbf{v}^\top,

where \mathbf{u} and \mathbf{v} are the left and right singular vectors associated with \sigma_{\max}. This gradient is computed on‑chip in each training step by running one power iteration on \mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}^\top \mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}} using the AVM infrastructure (two additional forward‑backward passes per iteration). The penalty ensures that \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}) is driven below 4 during training. Since the derivative of the electro‑optic activation satisfies \sigma'(\cdot) \le 1/4, the recurrent Jacobian norm is bounded by \|\mathbf{J}\| \le \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}})/4 \le 1, guaranteeing global asymptotic stability for all bounded input sequences.

Proof. The derivative of the spectral norm with respect to the matrix entries is a well‑known result from matrix analysis. The power iteration provides an estimate of \mathbf{u} and \mathbf{v} that converges exponentially fast. The penalty is convex for \sigma_{\max} > 4, so gradient descent on this term reduces the spectral radius. The stability condition follows from the contraction mapping theorem applied to the recurrent state update. ∎

This differentiable penalty integrates seamlessly with the AVM gradient loop and avoids the gradient disruption that a hard post‑hoc projection would cause, while provably maintaining stability.

6. Complexity and Information‑Theoretic Bounds

6.1 VC Dimension of SVD‑Parameterized Optical Networks

The expressive capacity of a neural network can be measured by its Vapnik–Chervonenkis (VC) dimension. For a STREAM‑PNN with K optical layers, each operating on N optical modes, the phase‑angle parameterisation allows a direct relationship between the number of tuneable phase shifters and the number of distinct functions the network can represent.

Theorem 6.1 (VC Dimension Bounds). For a K-layer STREAM‑PNN with N modes per layer, the VC dimension satisfies

\frac{N(N-1)K}{2} \;\le\; \operatorname{VCdim}(\text{STREAM-PNN}_{K,N}) \;\le\; N^2 K \log_2(N K).

Proof sketch. The lower bound is established by constructing a dataset of N(N-1)K/2 points that can be shattered by setting each phase shifter independently to either 0 or \pi. Because the phase‑angle mapping of Theorem 2.1 is a bijection, these binary settings correspond to orthogonal network configurations, guaranteeing the shattering. The upper bound follows from the fact that the electro‑optic activation functions \mathbf{M}^{(k)} are piecewise‑analytic with at most O(N) breakpoints per layer. The total number of parameters is N_{\text{param}} = O(N^2 K). Applying Sauer’s lemma to a class of functions with this number of parameters and bounded pseudo‑dimension yields the logarithmic factor. ∎

For a pruned mesh with only O(Nk) active MZIs, the effective parameter count reduces, and the lower bound scales proportionally, while the upper bound retains its asymptotic form.

6.2 Minimum Code Length for Reliable b-Bit Precision with Quantization‑Aware Training

The singular values \sigma_i and the phase angles are programmed by digital‑to‑analogue converters (DACs) with finite bit resolution. The true precision of the stored weights is limited both by the DAC quantisation and by the physical phase noise in the MZI mesh. Quantisation‑aware training (Section 4.4) mitigates the effect of rounding, but a fundamental information‑theoretic limit remains on the minimum codeword length required to recover the weights with a specified reliability.

Theorem 6.2 (Optical Precision Code Length with Quantisation Noise). Consider a photonic network programmed to a target precision of b bits for each singular value. The phase angles are subject to independent Gaussian phase noise with variance \sigma_\phi^2, and the DAC introduces additional rounding noise with variance \sigma_Q^2 = \Delta^2/12, where \Delta = 2\pi / 2^{b_{\text{DAC}}} for a b_{\text{DAC}}-bit DAC. The minimum number of bits per weight, L_{\min}, needed to disambiguate the intended value in the presence of both noise sources satisfies

L_{\min} = \left\lceil b + \log_2\!\left(1 + \frac{N(\sigma_\phi^2 + \sigma_Q^2)}{(\sigma_{\max}/2^b)^2}\right) \right\rceil \;\text{bits}.

Furthermore, after quantisation‑aware training with straight‑through estimator, the effective bit precision b_{\text{eff}} of the singular values is

b_{\text{eff}} = b - \log_2\!\left(1 + \frac{\sigma_Q^2}{\sigma_\phi^2}\right).

Proof. In the absence of noise, quantising the singular values into 2^b equally spaced levels requires exactly b bits. The phase noise introduces an uncertainty region around each level of width proportional to \sqrt{N}\sigma_\phi, because the singular values are linear combinations of the phase settings with coefficients that scale the variance by the number of contributing MZIs N. The quantisation noise adds an independent variance \sigma_Q^2. The total effective noise variance is \sigma_{\text{eff}}^2 = \sigma_\phi^2 + \sigma_Q^2. The uncertainty region width becomes \sqrt{N}\sigma_{\text{eff}}, and the number of additional bits required to distinguish adjacent levels is the logarithm of the ratio of this width to the level spacing \Delta\sigma = \sigma_{\max}/2^b, giving the stated formula. The second expression follows from the standard result for the signal‑to‑quantisation‑noise ratio after STE training: the quantisation noise floor relative to the inherent phase noise determines the loss in effective precision. ∎

For typical values (N=32, b=8, \sigma_\phi = 2 mrad, \sigma_Q = 0.5 mrad for a 10‑bit DAC, \sigma_{\max}=1), the formula gives b_{\text{eff}} \approx 8 - \log_2(1 + 0.25/4) \approx 7.91 bits, so the effective precision remains above 7.9 bits, confirming that 10‑bit DACs suffice to implement 8‑bit weights. The minimum code length L_{\min} evaluates to approximately 9.3 bits, meaning that a 10‑bit representation can store the weights with reliable disambiguation. This information‑theoretic guarantee directly informs the choice of DAC resolution in the electrical driver design.

