7. Conclusion

The STREAM‑PNN framework establishes a rigorous and comprehensive mathematical foundation for the design, training, and fabrication‑aware optimisation of electro‑optic neural network accelerators. By interleaving photonic matrix‑vector multiplications with low‑power electro‑optic amplitude modulations in an operator‑splitting architecture, the number of optical‑to‑electronic conversions is halved while preserving the full expressivity of deep ReLU networks. This central insight is supported by a suite of theorems that collectively address every critical aspect of realising such a system in integrated photonics.

The singular value decomposition of weight matrices and the bijective phase‑angle equivalence (Theorem 2.1) enable direct gradient‑based training on the physical chip. The end‑to‑end optical power budget with amplified spontaneous emission noise and its closed‑form maximum‑depth solution (Theorem 2.2) provide a quantitative design rule for amplifier placement and guarantee that signal integrity is maintained across the full network depth. Hardware‑aware pruning under spatial fabrication error correlations (Theorem 2.3) reduces the active MZI count from O(N^2) to O(N\log N) for typical spectral decays, while the fault‑tolerant three‑splitter MZI and hybrid volatile/phase‑change memory scheme (Section 2.4) ensure resilience against both hard defects and limited write endurance. The error propagation bounds and self‑configuration algorithms (Theorems 2.5, 2.6) set manufacturable fabrication tolerances and enable autonomous calibration.

The quantitative operator‑splitting approximation theorem (Theorem 3.1) proves that any deep ReLU network can be mapped onto a STREAM‑PNN with explicit depth, gain, and error bounds, and the balanced coherent detection scheme with APD‑assisted local oscillator distribution (Theorem 5.1.2) guarantees that signed pre‑activation values are recovered with negligible energy overhead, even in dense WDM configurations.

On the learning theory side, the in‑situ adjoint variable method with its full five‑term noise model (Theorem 4.1) permits gradient computation using only optical forward and backward passes, with a complete characterisation of the noise budget. The loss‑landscape analysis in the weakly nonlinear regime, combined with an efficiently computable curriculum training schedule via on‑chip Hessian power iteration (Theorem 4.2), provides a convergence guarantee to global minima without expensive digital computation. Fabrication‑aware PAC‑Bayes generalisation (Theorem 4.3) translates spatial process variations directly into a structured L₂ regulariser, enabling chips to be trained in a way that anticipates manufacturing imperfections. Photonic elastic weight consolidation with block‑diagonal Fisher information (Algorithm 4.5, Theorem 4.5.1) addresses the crucial edge‑AI requirement of continual learning across sequential tasks, with a capacity‑based forgetting bound that is independent of the number of tasks up to a well‑defined limit.

The architectural specification translates these theorems into concrete tier‑by‑tier targets: modulators with 50 GHz bandwidth, fault‑tolerant MZIs with Sb₂Se₃ non‑volatile memory, balanced coherent receivers with APD gain, regularised AWG pre‑compensation for WDM crosstalk, a fractal interconnect topology with bounded loss and latency, and a differentiable spectral‑norm penalty for stable recurrent extensions (Theorem 5.5). The energy roadmap demonstrates sub‑nanojoule inference in the current generation and a clear technology trajectory toward low‑picojoule operation with emerging sub‑threshold CMOS, all while maintaining sub‑nanosecond latency.

The information‑theoretic bounds (Chapter 6) close the loop by quantifying the expressive capacity and the minimum digital precision required, confirming that 10‑bit DACs suffice for 8‑bit weight fidelity and that the VC dimension scales favourably with the number of active parameters.

Taken together, these results form a complete, fabrication‑ready mathematical blueprint for scalable, trainable, and robust optical neural networks. The convergence of rigorous theorem proving and hardware‑algorithm co‑design positions STREAM‑PNN as a viable and compelling path toward sub‑nanosecond, ultra‑low‑energy edge AI inference, moving integrated photonic neural accelerators from laboratory demonstrations into practical, deployable hardware. Future work will address experimental validation on foundry‑fabricated test chips, the extension to convolutional and attention‑based architectures via photonic tensor cores, and the integration of STREAM‑PNN with near‑sensor processing pipelines for fully autonomous edge perception systems.

