A.1 Proof of Theorem 2.1 (Phase‑Angle Equivalence)

Let \mathbf{U} \in U(N) be a target unitary matrix. The Reck‑Zeilinger decomposition constructs \mathbf{U} as a product of L = N(N-1)/2 two‑dimensional unitary rotations T_{ij}(\theta_{ij},\phi_{ij}), each implemented by an MZI. The nulling procedure proceeds column‑by‑column from left to right and, within each column, from bottom to top. At step (i,j) with i > j, we consider the (i,j) entry of the partially processed matrix \mathbf{U}^{(j)}. The rotation

T_{ij}(\theta,\phi) = \begin{pmatrix} \sin\theta\, e^{i\phi} & \cos\theta \\ \cos\theta & -\sin\theta\, e^{i\phi} \end{pmatrix}

acting on rows j and i is chosen so that the new (i,j) entry vanishes:

\cos\theta \cdot U^{(j)}_{jj} - \sin\theta\, e^{i\phi} \cdot U^{(j)}_{ij} = 0.

This equation determines \theta_{ij} and \phi_{ij} uniquely up to a global phase. Explicitly,

\phi_{ij} = \arg\left( \frac{U^{(j)}_{jj}}{U^{(j)}_{ij}} \right), \qquad
\theta_{ij} = \arctan\!\left( \frac{|U^{(j)}_{jj}|}{|U^{(j)}_{ij}|} \right).

Because the nulling is deterministic and reversible—given \mathbf{U} one computes the angles sequentially, and given the angles one reconstructs \mathbf{U}—the mapping from the set of N(N-1) phase angles to the unitary matrix is a bijection onto the set of unitaries reachable by the mesh (which is all of U(N) for the triangular layout). An identical construction holds for \mathbf{V}. The singular values \{\sigma_i\} are encoded independently via the attenuation tier. Thus the complete parameter set (\{\phi_{ij}^U\},\{\sigma_i\},\{\phi_{ij}^V\}) is in one‑to‑one correspondence with the weight matrix \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^\dagger. ∎

