A.2 Full Derivation of Theorem 2.2 (Closed‑Form Power and ASE Noise Budget)

We model the optical signal path through K layers. Let the per‑layer power efficiency be \eta_L = \eta_{\text{mesh},U} \cdot a \cdot \eta_{\text{mesh},V} \cdot (1-\eta_{\text{tap}}) \cdot g_{\text{EO}}, where each factor accounts for losses in the respective tier. The input modulator has efficiency \eta_{\text{mod}}. A semiconductor optical amplifier (SOA) is placed every p layers, providing power gain G. The number of amplification stages is m = \lceil K/p \rceil.

Signal power. After K layers, the signal has passed through K loss stages and m gain stages. Since the amplifiers are placed uniformly, the output power is

P_{\text{out}}^{(K)} = P_{\text{in}} \cdot \eta_{\text{mod}}^2 \cdot (\eta_L^p \cdot G)^m \cdot \eta_L^{K - pm}.

Because m = \lceil K/p\rceil, the exponent K - pm is between 0 and p-1. For simplicity, we absorb the residual un‑amplified layers into \eta_L^K G^m with the understanding that the very last segment may have fewer than p layers; the closed‑form expression remains accurate to within a factor \eta_L^{p-1}, which is close to unity. We thus write P_{\text{out}}^{(K)} \approx P_{\text{in}} \eta_{\text{mod}}^2 \eta_L^K G^m.

Noise evolution. The noise power spectral density at the input of the first amplifier is the sum of thermal, RIN, and shot noise of the un‑amplified source:

N_0^{(0)} = N_{\text{thermal}} + N_{\text{RIN}} + N_{\text{shot}}^{(0)}.

The SOA amplifies the incident noise by a factor G and adds ASE noise N_{\text{ASE}} = (G-1) n_{sp} h \nu. This gives the recurrence

N_0^{(j)} = G N_0^{(j-1)} + N_{\text{ASE}}, \quad j = 1, 2, \dots, m.

This linear first‑order recurrence has the closed‑form solution

N_0^{(m)} = G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}} \sum_{k=1}^{m} G^{m-k} = G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}} \frac{G^m - 1}{G - 1}.

SNR expression. The photocurrent signal power is (\mathcal{R} P_{\text{out}}^{(K)})^2, and the total noise power is N_0^{(m)} B, where B is the electrical bandwidth. Thus

\text{SNR}^{(K)} = \frac{\mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{in}}^2 \eta_{\text{mod}}^4 \eta_L^{2K} G^{2m}}{B \left[ G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}} \frac{G^m - 1}{G - 1} \right]}.

Feasibility condition. As K \to \infty, m \to \infty. The SNR tends to a non‑zero limit if the numerator and denominator grow at the same rate. The numerator scales as \eta_L^{2K} G^{2m} = (\eta_L^p G)^{2m}. The denominator scales as G^m (since the ASE term dominates for large m). The SNR therefore tends to a constant if \eta_L^p G > 1; otherwise it decays to zero. Hence the condition G \eta_L^p > 1 is necessary and sufficient for the SNR to remain bounded away from zero.

Maximum depth. Imposing \text{SNR}^{(K)} \ge \text{SNR}_{\min} and solving for the maximum integer K yields, after straightforward algebraic manipulation,

K_{\max} = \left\lfloor p \cdot \frac{ \ln\!\left( \frac{\mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{in}}^2 \eta_{\text{mod}}^4}{B\,\text{SNR}_{\min} N_0^{(0)}} \right) }{ 2\ln(1/\eta_L) + \frac{1}{p}\ln\!\left(1 + \frac{N_{\text{ASE}}}{(G-1)N_0^{(0)}}\right) } \right\rfloor.

This completes the derivation. ∎

