A.3 Proof Sketch of Theorem 2.3 (Hardware‑Aware Pruning with Spatial Covariance)

The full proof proceeds in three steps.

Step 1: Householder representation. The rank‑k approximation \mathbf{U}_k is written as a product of k Householder reflections \mathbf{H}_1 \mathbf{H}_2 \dots \mathbf{H}_k, where \mathbf{H}_\ell = \mathbf{I} - 2 \mathbf{v}_\ell \mathbf{v}_\ell^\dagger / \|\mathbf{v}_\ell\|^2 and \mathbf{v}_\ell nulls the \ell-th column of \mathbf{W}_k. Under the spectral gap condition \sigma_k / \sigma_{k+1} > \eta_{\text{thr}}, the singular vectors are well‑separated and the Householder vectors \mathbf{v}_\ell are approximately sparse, with support concentrated on a small number of entries.

Step 2: Stability under correlated errors. The fabrication error field is modelled as a Gaussian process with covariance \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{fab}}. The perturbation to the Householder vector due to errors in the previously nulled columns is bounded by the condition number of the Gram matrix, which is controlled by the spectral gap. The threshold \eta_{\text{thr}} is derived from the requirement that the condition number remains below a constant, which depends on the largest eigenvalue \bar{\sigma}_\theta^2 = \lambda_{\max}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{fab}}). When the gap exceeds this threshold, the fill‑in (creation of new non‑zero entries) remains bounded, and the number of non‑zero entries in \mathbf{v}_\ell scales as O(N).

Step 3: MZI count. Each reflection \mathbf{H}_\ell requires a column of O(N) MZIs in the triangular mesh (since only the MZIs in the path of the non‑zero entries of \mathbf{v}_\ell need be activated). With k reflections, the total number of active MZIs is O(kN). For polynomially decaying singular values with exponent \alpha > 1/2, k = O(\log N), giving O(N\log N) complexity. ∎

---

A.4 Derivation of Theorem 2.5 (MZI Mesh Error Bound with Correlations)

The implemented unitary is \mathbf{U}_{\text{actual}} = \prod_{\ell=1}^{L} T_\ell(\theta_\ell + \delta\theta_\ell, \phi_\ell). Expanding to first order in \delta\theta_\ell,

\mathbf{U}_{\text{actual}} \approx \mathbf{U}_{\text{ideal}} + \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \frac{\partial \mathbf{U}_{\text{ideal}}}{\partial \theta_\ell} \delta\theta_\ell.

Let \mathbf{J}_\ell = \partial \mathbf{U}_{\text{ideal}} / \partial \theta_\ell. The error matrix is \Delta\mathbf{U} = \sum_\ell \mathbf{J}_\ell \delta\theta_\ell. The Frobenius norm squared is

\|\Delta\mathbf{U}\|_F^2 = \sum_{\ell,\ell'} \operatorname{tr}( \mathbf{J}_\ell^\dagger \mathbf{J}_{\ell'} ) \delta\theta_\ell \delta\theta_{\ell'}.

Taking expectations and using \mathbb{E}[\delta\theta_\ell \delta\theta_{\ell'}] = (\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta)_{\ell\ell'},

\mathbb{E}[\|\Delta\mathbf{U}\|_F^2] = \sum_{\ell,\ell'} \operatorname{tr}( \mathbf{J}_\ell^\dagger \mathbf{J}_{\ell'} ) (\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta)_{\ell\ell'} = \operatorname{tr}( \mathbf{J}^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta \mathbf{J} ),

where \mathbf{J} is the matrix whose columns are the vectorised \mathbf{J}_\ell. Bounding \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{J}_\ell^\dagger \mathbf{J}_\ell) \le 4 for each \ell (since \|\partial T_\ell/\partial\theta_\ell\|_F \le 2) and using the positive semi‑definiteness of \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta, we obtain \mathbb{E}[\|\Delta\mathbf{U}\|_F^2] \le 4 \operatorname{tr}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta) = 4 L \bar{\sigma}_\theta^2 \le 2 N^2 \bar{\sigma}_\theta^2. For the three‑splitter MZI, the sensitivity \partial T/\partial\theta is reduced, yielding a smaller constant. ∎

---

A.5 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Operator‑Splitting Approximation with Rate)

We approximate the ReLU activation by a scaled sigmoid: \text{ReLU}(z) \approx z \cdot \sigma(\alpha z) with error \epsilon_{\text{gate}}(\alpha) = \sup_{|z|\le R} |\text{ReLU}(z) - z\sigma(\alpha z)|. For large \alpha, this error satisfies \epsilon_{\text{gate}}(\alpha) \le R e^{-\alpha R} (since the sigmoid converges to the step function exponentially in the transition region). Given \varepsilon > 0, choose \alpha such that \epsilon_{\text{gate}}(\alpha) \le \varepsilon/(2K L), where L is the Lipschitz constant of the network. Each pair of ReLU layers is then approximated by a single sigmoidal mask after the composite matrix, with error bounded by 2\epsilon_{\text{gate}}. The total error across K layers is at most 2K \epsilon_{\text{gate}} \le \varepsilon/L, which after accounting for the L-Lipschitz propagation yields final error \varepsilon. The number of STREAM layers is K, whereas the original network had 2K ReLU layers, hence the factor 2 reduction. The depth scaling K(\varepsilon, L, R) \le 2K / \log_2(1+2L/\varepsilon) follows from the exponential convergence of the sigmoid. The warm‑start matches the first‑order Taylor expansion of the ReLU pair at zero, ensuring the initial guess is already close to the optimum. ∎

---

A.6 Full Noise Model for Theorem 4.1 (AVM Gradient with ASE)

The photocurrent at the detector monitoring the gradient for MZI \ell is a linear combination of the forward field E_f and the adjoint field E_a. The measured current is I = I_{\text{sig}} + i_{\text{noise}}, where I_{\text{sig}} = \eta_{\text{det}} \operatorname{Re}[E_f E_a^*] is the desired gradient term, and i_{\text{noise}} contains contributions from:

· Shot noise: i_{\text{shot}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 2 q I_{\text{dc}} B), with I_{\text{dc}} the total DC photocurrent.
· RIN: i_{\text{RIN}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \text{RIN} \cdot I_{\text{dc}}^2 B).
· Thermal noise: i_{\text{thermal}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 4 k_B T B / R_{\text{TIA}}).
· TPA noise: i_{\text{TPA}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \kappa P_{\text{opt}}^2 B), where \kappa depends on the TPA coefficient and the effective interaction length.
· ASE beat noise: i_{\text{ASE}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 2 \mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{sig}} N_{\text{ASE}} B), arising from the beating of the signal with the ASE field.

The total gradient variance is the sum of these independent variances, giving the stated expression. For low optical power (<0.5 mW), the shot noise term dominates, and the other contributions can be safely neglected. ∎

---

A.7 Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Curriculum Schedule with On‑Chip Hessian Estimate)

At \boldsymbol{\alpha}=0, the network is a deep linear network with phase‑angle parameterisation. The loss function L(\boldsymbol{\phi}) has the property that all local minima are global and all saddle points are strict (Kawaguchi, 2016). The Hessian \mathbf{H}_0 at any critical point has a single zero eigenvalue corresponding to global scaling, and all other eigenvalues are bounded away from zero.

For small \boldsymbol{\alpha}, the activation \mathbf{M}^{(k)} is a smooth perturbation of the identity, with Lipschitz constant 1 + O(\|\boldsymbol{\alpha}\|). The Hessian changes continuously with \boldsymbol{\alpha}; by the implicit function theorem, critical points shift by O(\|\boldsymbol{\alpha}\|^2) and no new local minima can appear as long as \|\boldsymbol{\alpha}\| < \alpha^*, where \alpha^* depends on the condition number of the weight matrices.

The curriculum schedule \alpha(t) = \alpha_{\text{final}}(1 - e^{-t/\hat{\tau}}) ensures \alpha(t) \le \alpha_{\text{final}} for all t. Choosing \alpha_{\text{final}} \le \alpha^* guarantees the benign landscape persists. The time constant \hat{\tau} = 1/\hat{\lambda}_{\max} uses an estimate of the largest Hessian eigenvalue, obtained by power iteration. The power method converges geometrically with error ( \lambda_2/\lambda_{\max} )^p, so after p = O(1) iterations the estimate is within a constant factor of the true value. The resulting schedule error only affects the convergence rate by a constant factor, as the curriculum is robust to small variations in \tau. The SGD analysis with decreasing step size \eta_t = 1/\sqrt{t} on a strict saddle function yields \mathbb{E}[\|\nabla L\|^2] = O(1/t). ∎

