A.8 Derivation of PAC‑Bayes Bound (Theorem 4.3)

Let the prior P be \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{0}, \mathbf{K}_{\text{fab}}) and the posterior Q be \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\phi}^*, s^2 \mathbf{I}). The KL divergence between two multivariate Gaussians is

\operatorname{KL}(Q \| P) = \frac12 \left[ \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{K}_{\text{fab}}^{-1} (s^2 \mathbf{I})) + (\boldsymbol{\phi}^*)^\top \mathbf{K}_{\text{fab}}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\phi}^* - d - \ln \frac{\det(s^2 \mathbf{I})}{\det \mathbf{K}_{\text{fab}}} \right],

where d = N_{\text{param}}. Substituting \mathbf{K}_{\text{fab}} = \mathbf{L}\mathbf{L}^\top (Cholesky) gives \|\boldsymbol{\phi}^*\|_{\mathbf{K}_{\text{fab}}^{-1}} = \|\mathbf{L}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\phi}^*\|^2. The PAC‑Bayes theorem for a loss bounded by 1 (after scaling) states that with probability 1-\delta,

L_{\text{test}}(Q) \le L_{\text{train}}(Q) + \sqrt{ \frac{ \operatorname{KL}(Q\|P) + \ln(m/\delta) }{ 2m } }.

Minimising the right‑hand side with respect to s^2 and the regularisation parameter yields the spatially weighted L_2 regulariser \lambda \|\mathbf{L}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\phi}\|^2 with \lambda = 1/(2m). The structured penalty penalises directions corresponding to the dominant eigenvectors of \mathbf{K}_{\text{fab}} (low‑frequency spatial modes) more heavily, matching the physical reality that correlated errors are more harmful. ∎

---

A.9 Proof of Local Oscillator Power Budget (Theorem 5.1.2)

A balanced detector with LO power P_{\text{LO,ch}} produces a DC photocurrent I_{\text{LO}} = \eta_{\text{det}} P_{\text{LO,ch}} per photodiode. The shot noise variance for each diode is 2 q I_{\text{LO}} B; for two diodes in differential mode, the variance adds. The total shot noise at the differential output is \sigma_{\text{shot}}^2 = 4 q \eta_{\text{det}} P_{\text{LO,ch}} B (assuming the LO power is much larger than the signal power). The thermal noise variance of the TIA is \sigma_{\text{thermal}}^2 = 4 k_B T B / R_{\text{TIA}}. For shot‑noise‑limited operation we require \sigma_{\text{shot}}^2 \ge \sigma_{\text{thermal}}^2, yielding

4 q \eta_{\text{det}} P_{\text{LO,ch}} B \ge \frac{4 k_B T B}{R_{\text{TIA}}} \quad \Rightarrow \quad P_{\text{LO,ch}} \ge \frac{k_B T}{q \eta_{\text{det}} R_{\text{TIA}}}.

With an APD of multiplication gain M, the shot noise is multiplied by M^2 F(M) while the thermal noise is unchanged. The condition becomes

4 q \eta_{\text{det}} M^2 F(M) P_{\text{LO,ch}} B \ge \frac{4 k_B T B}{R_{\text{TIA}}} \quad \Rightarrow \quad P_{\text{LO,ch}} \ge \frac{k_B T}{q \eta_{\text{det}} M^2 F(M) R_{\text{TIA}}}.

Using M=10 and F(M) \approx 2, the required per‑channel LO power is reduced by a factor M^2/F(M) \approx 50. For R_{\text{TIA}} = 1\,\text{k}\Omega, T=300\,\text{K}, \eta_{\text{det}}=0.8, the requirement is P_{\text{LO,ch}} \ge 1.3\,\mu\text{W}. After 1{:}(W N) splitting, the LO source power is P_{\text{LO}} = P_{\text{LO,ch}} W N / \eta_{\text{split}}. With W=8, N=32, \eta_{\text{split}}=0.8, this gives P_{\text{LO}} \ge 0.42\,\text{mW}, easily met by a 1 mW laser. ∎

