A.10 Derivation of Spectral‑Norm Penalty Gradient (Theorem 5.5)

The penalty is \mathcal{P}(\mathbf{W}) = \lambda_{\text{stab}} \max(0, \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}) - 4)^2. For a matrix \mathbf{W} with singular value decomposition \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\top, where \sigma_{\max} is the largest singular value and \mathbf{u}_1, \mathbf{v}_1 are the corresponding left and right singular vectors, the gradient of \sigma_{\max} with respect to \mathbf{W} is \mathbf{u}_1 \mathbf{v}_1^\top. By the chain rule, for \sigma_{\max} > 4,

\nabla_{\mathbf{W}} \mathcal{P} = 2 \lambda_{\text{stab}} (\sigma_{\max} - 4) \cdot \mathbf{u}_1 \mathbf{v}_1^\top.

When \sigma_{\max} \le 4, the gradient is zero. This gradient can be computed via one step of power iteration on \mathbf{W}^\top \mathbf{W} to obtain approximate singular vectors, which is compatible with the AVM framework because the power iteration involves matrix‑vector products with \mathbf{W} and \mathbf{W}^\top, i.e., forward and adjoint propagation. The penalty is convex for \sigma_{\max} > 4, so gradient steps strictly reduce the spectral norm. The stability condition follows because the recurrent Jacobian is bounded by \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}) \cdot \sup \sigma' \le \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}})/4. If the penalty drives \sigma_{\max} < 4, the Jacobian norm is strictly less than 1, ensuring contractivity. ∎

