Appendix B: Architectural Diagrams and Design Rules

(This appendix is presented in textual form, describing the key diagrams and design rules.)

B.1 STREAM‑PNN Layer Block Diagram

A single STREAM‑PNN layer consists of five tiers arranged in series. The optical path is:

1. Input Encoding (Tier 1). An array of N electro‑optic modulators (ring resonators for single‑wavelength, or multi‑wavelength rings for WDM) encodes the N-dimensional input vector \mathbf{x} onto the complex amplitudes of N optical modes. Each modulator has a bandwidth \ge 50 GHz, V_\pi \le 2 V, and insertion loss \le 1.5 dB.
2. Unitary Mesh \mathbf{U} (Tier 2). A triangular mesh of N(N-1)/2 MZIs. In active regions, three‑splitter MZIs are used for fault tolerance; in pruned (idle) regions, standard two‑coupler MZIs are set to the identity state. Each active MZI arm contains a hybrid weight cell: a non‑volatile phase‑change material (Sb₂Se₃) segment for long‑term storage and a thermo‑optic heater for rapid training updates. The mesh implements the unitary matrix \mathbf{U} of the SVD. Phase shifter resolution is \ge 10 bits, tuning efficiency \le 0.1 mW/\pi, thermal crosstalk \le -30 dB.
3. Singular Value Scaling (Tier 3). A row of N variable optical attenuators (p‑i‑n junction carrier‑injection waveguides) implements the diagonal matrix \boldsymbol{\Sigma}. Attenuation range 0–20 dB, resolution 10 bits, response time \le 500 ns. Optional PCM storage for long‑term retention.
4. Unitary Mesh \mathbf{V}^\dagger (Tier 4). Architecturally identical to Tier 2, implementing the conjugate transpose \mathbf{V}^\dagger. The same pruning rules and hybrid storage apply.
5. Electro‑Optic Activation (Tier 5). The output of \mathbf{V}^\dagger is split by a 50/50 coupler with a local oscillator for balanced coherent detection. The resulting signed pre‑activation z_i is converted to a modulation coefficient m_i = \sigma(\alpha_i z_i + \beta_i) via a 6‑bit DAC and look‑up table. The modulation coefficient is impressed onto the optical carrier by an amplitude modulator. A 10 % tap of the light is used for gradient measurement. Variant A (single‑quadrature) uses 2 detectors/channel; Variant B (full IQ) uses 4 detectors/channel. APDs with gain M \approx 10 ensure shot‑noise‑limited operation. The electrical chain latency is <200 ps.

B.2 Hybrid Weight Cell Schematic

Each active MZI arm contains:

· A short section of Sb₂Se₃ phase‑change material, contacted by two electrodes for electrical programming. The amorphous/crystalline state determines the static phase shift \Delta\phi_{\text{PCM}} with >7‑bit precision. The PCM state is set once per task and requires zero static hold power.
· A resistive micro‑heater (TiN or NiCr) placed over a portion of the silicon waveguide, which provides rapid, fine‑tuned phase adjustment \Delta\phi_{\text{thermo}} during training. The heater is controlled by a current DAC.
· The total phase shift is the sum \Delta\phi = \Delta\phi_{\text{PCM}} + \Delta\phi_{\text{thermo}}.

During inference, \Delta\phi_{\text{thermo}} = 0 and the heater is turned off. The PCM retains its state indefinitely. During training, gradient updates are applied to the heater DAC; the PCM is reprogrammed only at the conclusion of the task.

B.3 Balanced Coherent Detection (Variant A)

The signal from the \mathbf{V}^\dagger mesh, E_{\text{sig}}, and the local oscillator, E_{\text{LO}}, are combined on a 50/50 directional coupler. The two outputs illuminate a balanced pair of germanium APDs. The differential photocurrent is

I_{\Delta} = \mathcal{R} \cdot 2 \operatorname{Re}[ E_{\text{sig}} E_{\text{LO}}^* ].

A slow phase‑locked loop (bandwidth ~1 MHz) adjusts the LO phase to track the mean signal phase, ensuring the quadrature containing the signal is maximised. The LO distribution network uses a symmetric splitter tree with inverse‑taper crossings to minimise loss.

B.4 Fractal Topology Layout

At depth d=0, a single tile of size N \times N is used. At depth d=1, the matrix is partitioned into 2 tiles of size N/2 \times N/2 (if N is even) and interconnected by a cross‑connect of size N \times N. The cross‑connect is itself an MZI mesh that obeys the same pruning rules. The pattern repeats recursively. To reduce waveguide crossings, signals from one layer are routed to a SiN over‑cladding layer via vertical grating couplers (loss ~0.1 dB per transition). The fractal topology achieves O(N_{\text{eff}} \log N_{\text{eff}}) active MZI count and O(d \log N) crossing loss.

